<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Poona Pact</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Poona</a> Pact was an agreement of 24 September 1932 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> on the political representation of the depressed classes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, now known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-castes">Scheduled Castes</a>. It answered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communal-award">Communal Award</a> of August 1932, which had granted the depressed classes separate electorates; Gandhi, imprisoned in Yerwada Central Jail in Poona, began a fast unto death against it, seeing it as a British attempt to divide the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>. The pact, signed by 23 people, replaced separate electorates with 148 seats reserved for the depressed classes within joint electorates, nearly twice what the Award had offered. Its reserved seats endure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s legislatures.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Seats in legislative bodies were first allocated by identity under the Indian Councils Act of 1909, and the depressed classes received some seats in 1919, increased in 1925. During the Second Round Table Conference the British government proposed separate electorates for the depressed classes, Muslims, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, Indian Christians and others, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communal-award">Communal Award</a> of Prime Minister Ramsay MacDonald followed in August 1932. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> accepted separate electorates for the other groups but not for the depressed classes, writing that they would vivisect and disrupt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a>. B. R. Ambedkar argued that upper-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> reformers could not represent the depressed classes, who needed leaders of their own.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The pact was signed at 5 pm on 24 September 1932 at Yerwada Central Jail, with Madan Mohan Malaviya signing on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s son Devdas Gandhi among the signatories. The reserved seats were divided among the provinces: 30 each in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, 25 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, 20 each in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-provinces">Central Provinces</a> and the United Provinces, 18 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Orissa</a>, 8 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and 7 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. In each constituency the depressed-class voters on the general roll would first choose a panel of four candidates, from whom the joint electorate would elect the member.</p><p>The same system applied to the Central Legislature, where 18 per cent of the general seats for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> were reserved. Ambedkar had proposed that the primary elections end automatically after ten years, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> a referendum after five; the compromise ended the primaries after ten years unless the communities agreed otherwise. The pact also barred any disability in local elections or public appointments on account of membership of the depressed classes, and earmarked part of each province's education grant for them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>8 January 1933 was observed as Temple Entry Day. The pact set <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s view of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> reform as a social and spiritual task against Ambedkar's insistence that caste was a political question. Writers such as Perry Anderson and Arundhati Roy have asked whether Gandhi coerced Ambedkar, while the historian Gitanjali Surendran holds that Ambedkar was content with the outcome.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Poona Pact” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>B. R. Ambedkar (1891–1956), the foremost leader of the untouchable castes, clashed with Gandhi in 1932, when Gandhi fasted against separate electorates for the depressed classes until Ambedkar accepted reserved seats instead in the <b>Poona Pact</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · People</a></li><li><blockquote>B. R. Ambedkar, born into a caste classified as untouchable, became a leader of human-rights campaigns; at his request the Communal Award of 1932 gave the depressed classes separate electorates, which he gave up in the <b>Poona Pact</b> after Gandhi’s hunger strike.</blockquote><a href="/societies/british-raj">Social Hierarchy of British India</a></li><li><blockquote>Agreed with Gandhi during his fast, the <b>Poona Pact</b> was signed, replacing separate electorates for the 'depressed classes' with reserved seats.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
